{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghwDFxnKz7Nr7HSSPY2f","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I solved this with the usual closed-form expressions for the quartet levels of high-spin octahedral d⁵, measured from the ⁶A₁ ground state. These expressions are approximate, so Dq is the least reliable result; see the caveat at the end.\n\n**Approximate level energies**\n\n- E(⁴A₁, ⁴E(G)) = 10B + 5C. This does not depend on Dq.\n- E(⁴T₁(G)) ≈ 10B + 6C − 10Dq − 26B²/(10Dq)\n- E(⁴T₂(G)) ≈ 18B + 6C − 10Dq − 38B²/(10Dq)\n\n**Step 1: use the ⁴E band to link B and C**\n\n10B + 5C = 24 980, so C = 4996 − 2B.\n\n**Step 2: substitute into the two ⁴T bands**\n\nWrite x = 10Dq.\n\n- From the ⁴T₁ band at 19 720: x + 26B²/x = 10 256 − 2B\n- From the ⁴T₂ band at 23 260: x + 38B²/x = 6716 + 6B\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second gives 12B²/x = 8B − 3540, so x = 12B²/(8B − 3540).\n\n**Step 3: solve for B numerically**\n\nSubstituting x back into the ⁴T₁ equation and solving gives:\n\n- B ≈ 788 cm⁻¹\n- 10Dq ≈ 2 696 cm⁻¹, so Dq ≈ 270 cm⁻¹\n- C ≈ 3 420 cm⁻¹, giving C/B ≈ 4.3\n\n**Caveat**\n\n- **B and C** are well determined. The ⁴E/⁴A₁ band fixes 10B + 5C directly, and the values are typical for Mn²⁺.\n- **Dq** depends on the shortcut formulas. They are second-order expressions from the strong-field limit, while Mn²⁺ is in the weak-field regime. They also leave out mixing with other configurations, such as the C²/(20Dq) term from the ⁴T₁ levels of the t₂²e³ configuration. As a result, Dq comes out about a third of the usual value of roughly 750 cm⁻¹ for Mn²⁺ in a fluoride lattice. To get a more reliable Dq, fit the bands by diagonalising the full Tanabe–Sugano ⁴T₁ and ⁴T₂ matrices instead.\n\n{\"Dq\": 270, \"B\": 788, \"C\": 3420, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Fitted with approximate d5 quartet formulas; B and C are reliable, but Dq is likely underestimated.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,